{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nGit failure strings are classified at five sites, with different verdicts for the same text: `_store/gitutil.py:215,221,517` (index-lock + transient-FS, retried locally); `_store/push_classify.py:48,61,110` (non-FF / policy-decline / transport); `_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock_push.py:68,74` and `_advisory_lock.py:285` (lease/CAS, terminal); `_store/event_append.py:465` (invalid-object recovery). `cannot lock ref` gets three different verdicts.\n\nThe synthetic rc-124 `git timed out after` result is built by FOUR places — `push.py:71 _git`, `sync.py:66 _git`, `event_commit_git.py:64 _run_git`, `gitutil.py:604` — and is retriable to only one classifier. `wrongful-chemic-squeaker` added the transient retry to `s3_doctor` only, leaving `sync._union_merge` (sync.py:174) and `push_recovery`'s merges (push_recovery.py:263) without it.\n\n## Approach\nAdd `src/rebar/_store/git_outcome.py`: one marker registry and `classify(result, *, operation) -> GitOutcome` over ten kinds — `LOCK, TRANSIENT_FS, TRANSPORT, NON_FF, CAS_MISMATCH, POLICY_DECLINE, DIRTY_WD, UNMERGED, INVALID_OBJECT, FATAL` — with the subtractive \"policy wins over non-FF\" rule applied once. Existing predicates become lookups; marker tables move, unchanged.\n\n`gitutil.py` gains `run_git_bounded(...)` — the ONE construction of that rc-124 result — and the three shims plus `run_git_write._bounded_once` delegate to it. Two owners because these are two constructs: `git_outcome.py` owns the marker STRINGS, `gitutil.py` the timeout CONSTRUCTION (where the shared runner already lives; moving `run_git` is out of scope).\n\n## Guard construction\nPer the parent epic `airborne-wellloved-kingfisher`: owner exempt, match the AST shape named here, `# <construct>-ok: <reason>` escape with mandatory reason.\nGuard A (markers) — over `_store/*.py` + `_engine/rebar_reconciler/*.py`, owner `git_outcome.py` exempt: no non-docstring string LITERAL (AST `Constant`, so comment/docstring prose is ignored) may contain `index.lock`, `non-fast-forward`, `cannot lock ref`, or `could not parse head`. Escape `# git-marker-ok: <reason>`; two are legitimate — `_LOCK_REF_RE` parses the ref name for the exhaustion hint, `_reclaim_if_stale_index_lock` joins the lock PATH.\n\nGuard B (timeout) — over `_store/*.py`, owner `gitutil.py` exempt: no `subprocess.CompletedProcess` call may carry BOTH the int literal `124` AND a str/f-string argument containing `timed out after`. That conjunction distinguishes a construction from the `\"git timed out after\"` marker row, a legitimate table entry. Escape `# git-timeout-ok: <reason>`.\n\n## Constraint: the classifier signature\n`classify(result, *, operation) -> GitOutcome`. The five `operation` values keying the registry are exactly `\"local\"` (gitutil store-local ops), `\"commit\"` (event_append's lock-held commit), `\"push\"` (push.py's network push), `\"lease-push\"` (the reconciler's `--force-with-lease` ref push), `\"ref-cas\"` (its `update-ref` CAS). The key is a PAIR because a marker alone is ambiguous: the same string means a retriable local lock, a non-CAS push reject, or a held advisory lock depending on the operation. And some verdicts are not marker-based — `_is_cas_mismatch` discriminates on command shape and exit code (`\"update-ref\" in args and ref_name in args`, then `returncode == 128` OR `returncode == 1` with `\"cannot lock ref\" in stderr`), which no `(marker, operation) -> kind` TABLE expresses. So the registry admits two entry kinds: a **marker row** (common case) and a **structural predicate** registered for an operation.\n\nData flow: `classify` accepts a `CompletedProcess`, a `CalledProcessError`, or a bare `str`, reading the failure text via `getattr` — no shape conversion. A structural predicate runs FIRST for its operation, handed the object verbatim, self-guarding on type so a non-matching shape falls through to the marker rows. `_is_cas_mismatch` moves in as the `ref-cas` predicate **verbatim**, still taking the `CalledProcessError` it takes today. `_advisory_lock` re-exports it under the same name and `_cas_once` KEEPS calling it directly rather than via `classify`, so `_ref_lock`'s `acquire/release/steal/renew` seams are untouched; `classify(exc, operation=\"ref-cas\")` is the registry entry the table test drives. Both paths run the one function.\n\n## Constraint: `cannot lock ref` MUST NOT be merged\nThree deliberately different, bug-hardened verdicts: under `local` (`_is_git_lock_error`) a LOCAL ref-lock conflict, ridden out; under `lease-push` (`_ref_lock_push.py:68`) in `PUSH_REJECT_MARKERS` but explicitly NOT a lease mismatch — **bug 4afc** established only `stale info` is the `--force-with-lease` signal, since `cannot lock ref` also covers hook declines, rate limits, server errors and remote ref.lock contention; **bug ebee** added `fatal error in commit_refs` to that non-CAS set; under `ref-cas` (`_advisory_lock.py:305`) with exit 128 or exit 1 it means the advisory lock is HELD (terminal).\n\nCollapsing these would re-open 4afc and ebee. The divergence is PRESERVED; the guard proves the strings live in one module, not that they carry one verdict.\n\n## Constraint: the tenth kind, INVALID_OBJECT\n`event_append.py:465` `_INVALID_OBJECT_MARKERS = (\"invalid object\", \"error building trees\")` is git `write-tree` refusing a commit because an index entry references an object MISSING from the object DB. Its handler is a **recovery** (rebuild the index, retry), not a terminal failure, so folding it into `FATAL` would delete that recovery. Hence a tenth kind, `_is_invalid_object_error` a lookup against it.\n\n## Scope\n`src/rebar/_store/`: `git_outcome.py` (new), `gitutil.py`, `push_classify.py`, `push.py`, `sync.py`, `push_recovery.py`, `event_commit_git.py`, `event_append.py`, `s3_doctor.py`; `_engine/rebar_reconciler/`: `_ref_lock_push.py`, `_advisory_lock.py`; `tests/unit/store/test_git_outcome.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\nTable-driven test: one captured stderr per kind maps to exactly one kind under its operation. Edge: a stderr carrying both `rejected` and a policy-decline marker classifies POLICY_DECLINE. Pinning suites, unedited: `tests/unit/` `test_gitutil_transient_write_retry.py`, `test_lock_retry_zebrafish.py`, `test_lock_stale_reclaim.py`, `test_push_rejection_surfacing.py`, `test_push_policy.py`; `tests/integration/test_s3_doctor*.py`; `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_ref_lock*.py`, `test_advisory_lock*.py`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every current marker string resolves to one kind for a given operation; checked by the table test.\n- [ ] Each caller's kind-to-action mapping is unchanged; checked by the pinning suites, unedited.\n- [ ] `sync._union_merge` retries a TRANSIENT_FS fault through the shared retry; checked by a test injecting `could not parse HEAD` once and asserting the merge then succeeds.\n- [ ] `push_recovery`'s recovery merge does the same; checked by that injection against `_merge_with_transport_retry`.\n- [ ] Guard A passes: the four marker strings appear as string literals only in `git_outcome.py`, modulo reasoned escapes.\n- [ ] Guard B passes: zero synthetic-timeout constructions in `src/rebar/_store` outside `gitutil.py`, exactly one inside it.\n- [ ] The three shims are deleted, their callers using `gitutil.run_git_bounded`; checked by Guard B plus the pinning suites.\n- [ ] `cannot lock ref` keeps its three distinct verdicts; checked by a table test asserting each operation's verdict for that one string.\n- [ ] `_is_cas_mismatch`'s exit-code + command-shape discrimination is preserved exactly (exit 128, or exit 1 with `cannot lock ref`, only for an `update-ref` naming the ref); checked by a test driving all four combinations.\n- [ ] `INVALID_OBJECT` is a distinct kind whose action remains index-rebuild-then-retry; checked by a test injecting `invalid object` into an event append and asserting it then succeeds via the rebuild path."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787377568813619001,"uuid":"6997e918-6257-4aa9-8b1c-6e42db30d094"}